---
import { insuranceSurveyPage } from '../data/insurance-survey';
import ServiceLayout from '../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import { createServiceSchema } from '../utils/seo';

const serviceSchema = createServiceSchema({
  name: 'Insurance Condition Yacht Survey',
  serviceType: 'Insurance condition yacht survey',
  pathname: insuranceSurveyPage.pathname,
  description: insuranceSurveyPage.schemaDescription,
  areaServed: insuranceSurveyPage.areaServed,
});
---

<ServiceLayout
  title={insuranceSurveyPage.title}
  description={insuranceSurveyPage.description}
  locale="en"
  routeId="insuranceSurvey"
  pathname={insuranceSurveyPage.pathname}
  heading={insuranceSurveyPage.heading}
  eyebrow={insuranceSurveyPage.eyebrow}
  summary={insuranceSurveyPage.summary}
  heroImage={insuranceSurveyPage.heroImage}
  socialImageAlt={insuranceSurveyPage.socialImageAlt}
  breadcrumbs={insuranceSurveyPage.breadcrumbs}
  primaryCta={insuranceSurveyPage.primaryCta}
  secondaryCta={insuranceSurveyPage.secondaryCta}
  relatedServices={insuranceSurveyPage.relatedServices}
  finalCta={insuranceSurveyPage.finalCta}
  structuredData={serviceSchema}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="independent-insurance-survey-heading">
    <h2 id="independent-insurance-survey-heading">
      Independent Insurance Condition Yacht Survey
    </h2>
    <p>
      An insurance condition survey provides an independent assessment of a
      yacht’s general condition, safety arrangements and visible defects for
      consideration by an insurance underwriter.
    </p>
    <p>
      Insurers may request a current survey before offering cover, renewing an
      existing policy or accepting a change of ownership. The exact requirements
      vary between insurers and may depend on the yacht’s age, value,
      construction, use, cruising area and claims history.
    </p>
    <p>
      All Yacht Service carries out insurance condition surveys for sailing
      yachts, motor yachts and catamarans up to 40 metres.
    </p>
    <p>
      Surveys are carried out by Aleksandrs Tolkacovs, an IIMS-Certified Yacht
      and Small Craft Marine Surveyor and licensed captain, supported by more
      than 20 years of experience in engineering, quality assurance and
      management.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="survey-required-heading">
    <h2 id="survey-required-heading">
      When an Insurance Survey May Be Required
    </h2>
    <p>
      An insurer or broker may request a condition survey in situations
      including:
    </p>
    <div class="inspection-grid">
      {
        insuranceSurveyPage.suitabilityItems.map((item) => (
          <article>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.description}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="service-note">
      The insurer decides whether a survey is required and what information must
      be included. Owners should obtain the insurer’s written survey
      requirements before the inspection is arranged whenever possible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="insurance-survey-includes-heading">
    <h2 id="insurance-survey-includes-heading">
      What an Insurance Condition Survey Includes
    </h2>
    <p>
      The exact scope is agreed according to the vessel and the insurer’s
      requirements. A typical insurance condition survey may include:
    </p>
    <div class="inspection-grid">
      {
        insuranceSurveyPage.inspectionItems.map((item) => (
          <article>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.description}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="service-note">
      The survey is a non-destructive inspection and is limited to areas,
      systems and equipment that are safely accessible at the time of the
      inspection. The survey does not guarantee that every existing defect will
      be identified, particularly where components or areas are inaccessible,
      concealed or not operating during the inspection.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="condition-findings-heading">
    <h2 id="condition-findings-heading">
      Condition, Safety and Insurability-Related Findings
    </h2>
    <p>
      The survey identifies visible defects and safety concerns that may be
      relevant to an insurer’s assessment of the yacht.
    </p>
    <p>Findings may include:</p>
    <ul class="defect-grid">
      {insuranceSurveyPage.findings.map((finding) => <li>{finding}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p class="service-note">
      The report records the observed condition and provides recommendations.
      The insurer remains solely responsible for deciding whether to offer
      cover, impose conditions, require repairs or decline the risk.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="insurer-requirements-heading">
    <h2 id="insurer-requirements-heading">
      Confirming the Insurer’s Requirements
    </h2>
    <p>
      Insurance companies do not all request the same survey format or
      inspection scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before arranging the survey, the owner should ask the insurer or broker
      whether they require:
    </p>
    <ul class="service-check-list">
      {
        insuranceSurveyPage.insurerRequirements.map((requirement) => (
          <li>{requirement}</li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      Any insurer-provided form or written instructions should be supplied to
      All Yacht Service before the survey appointment. This helps ensure that
      the agreed inspection scope addresses the underwriter’s stated
      requirements.
    </p>
    <p class="service-note">
      Forms that request testing, certification or specialist work outside the
      agreed surveyor’s competence or inspection scope may require input from
      another appropriately qualified professional.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="insurance-process-heading">
    <h2 id="insurance-process-heading">
      How the Insurance Survey Process Works
    </h2>
    <ol class="process-list">
      {
        insuranceSurveyPage.processSteps.map((step, index) => (
          <li>
            <div>
              <span aria-hidden="true">
                {String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
              </span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>{step.title}</h3>
              <p>{step.description}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="afloat-haul-out-heading">
    <h2 id="afloat-haul-out-heading">
      Afloat and Hauled-Out Insurance Surveys
    </h2>
    <p>
      Some insurers accept an afloat condition inspection, while others require
      the underwater hull and appendages to be examined with the yacht out of
      the water.
    </p>
    <p>
      A hauled-out inspection allows assessment of the underwater hull, keel,
      rudder, propellers, shafts, saildrives, skin fittings and other underwater
      components.
    </p>
    <p>
      The appropriate arrangement should be confirmed with the insurer before
      the survey wherever possible.
    </p>
    <p>
      Haul-out, marina, cleaning and vessel-operating costs are normally
      arranged separately and are not automatically included in the survey fee
      unless specifically stated in the quotation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="insurance-valuation-heading">
    <h2 id="insurance-valuation-heading">Insurance Valuation</h2>
    <p>
      An insurer may request an opinion of the yacht’s current market value or
      an agreed insured value as part of the reporting process.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where valuation is included in the agreed scope, it is based on available
      vessel information, observed condition, specifications, equipment and
      relevant market evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      A valuation is an informed professional opinion at the date of inspection.
      It is not a guarantee of the price the yacht will achieve if sold.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="insurance-report-heading">
    <h2 id="insurance-report-heading">
      Clear Report for the Owner and Insurer
    </h2>
    <p>
      The insurance survey report describes the yacht’s observed condition in
      clear and practical language.
    </p>
    <p>It may include:</p>
    <ul class="service-check-list">
      {insuranceSurveyPage.reportItems.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      Reports are normally issued within 48 hours after completion of the
      inspection, subject to the vessel, survey scope, insurer’s documentation
      and any additional information required.
    </p>
    <p class="service-note">
      The report is prepared for the commissioning client and the stated
      insurance purpose. It should not be treated as a warranty, guarantee of
      condition or confirmation that insurance cover will be provided.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="insurance-versus-purchase-heading">
    <h2 id="insurance-versus-purchase-heading">
      Insurance Survey or Pre-Purchase Survey?
    </h2>
    <p>
      An insurance condition survey is prepared primarily to provide an insurer
      with information about the yacht’s general condition, safety and
      insurability-related risks.
    </p>
    <p>
      A
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/pre-purchase-survey"
        >pre-purchase yacht survey</a
      >{' '}
      is commissioned by a prospective buyer and normally involves a broader assessment
      intended to support the purchase decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      An insurance survey should not automatically be treated as a substitute
      for a comprehensive pre-purchase survey.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="insurance-coverage-heading">
    <h2 id="insurance-coverage-heading">
      Insurance Yacht Surveys Across Spain and the Mediterranean
    </h2>
    <p>
      Based at Marina Greenwich in Altea, All Yacht Service carries out
      insurance condition yacht surveys throughout Alicante, Costa Blanca,
      Valencia, Barcelona, the Spanish Mediterranean coast and other locations
      across mainland Spain.
    </p>
    <p>
      Surveys in the Balearic Islands, France, Italy, Greece and other European
      or international locations can also be arranged according to the vessel’s
      location, access requirements and insurer’s survey scope.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="why-insurance-survey-heading">
    <h2 id="why-insurance-survey-heading">Why Choose All Yacht Service?</h2>
    <ul class="service-check-list">
      {insuranceSurveyPage.whyChoose.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>
</ServiceLayout>
